    'Classifies reciprocity, consistency, social proof, liking, authority, and scarcity as influence cues with ethical limits.',
    'When persuasion principles appear, tag the pressure and the cost. Reciprocity can be generosity or obligation bait. Consistency can be integrity or commitment trapping. Social proof can be community evidence or herd pressure. Liking can be warmth or cultivated dependency. Authority can be expertise or intimidation. Scarcity can be real constraint or urgency theater. The fiction value is the gap between what the tactic claims and what it does to the person receiving it.',
