    'Keeps deception, microexpression, gaze, baseline, and nonverbal-cue material useful without mind-reading certainty.',
    'Reading people works best as scene observation, not verdict. Store baseline, cue cluster, context, contradiction, timing, gaze, vocal change, and verbal inconsistency. Then preserve alternate explanations: fatigue, culture, disability, fear, neurotype, pain, social performance, lighting, relationship history, and observer bias. A character may believe a cue proves deception; the seed layer should keep the uncertainty alive until evidence resolves it.',
    array[
