    'physical-technique-cause-effect',
    'Physical reference',
    'Keeps technique readable through pressure, angle, rhythm, support, contact, reaction, and consequence.',
    'For any physical technique, write cause and effect. Name the contact, pressure, angle, rhythm, support, and body reaction. If rhythm changes, say what changed it: fatigue, better leverage, a corrected angle, a boundary, a verbal cue, a slipping support point, or a new emotional beat. Technique details should serve character desire, agency, comfort, and continuity rather than becoming disconnected body-part inventory.',
