  ),
  (
    'core-comm-code-consent-signal-map',
    'Comm Code Consent Signal Map',
    'comm-code-consent-signal-map',
    'Turns initiation, consent, refusal, stop, keep-going, and check-in language into reusable intimacy communication fields.',
    'A communication code should store the actual words or signals each partner recognizes: how they initiate, how they say yes, how they say no, what stop sounds like, what keep going sounds like, what check on me looks like, and which names or terms feel good or bad during sex. In fiction, these details make consent feel human: a familiar phrase, a hand signal, a joke that means slow down, or a name that suddenly matters.',
    'Do not make coded signals obscure, humiliating, or easy to miss unless the scene is explicitly about a communication failure and its consequences.',
    array[
      'comm-code-consent-language',
      'initiation-consent-refusal-phrases',
      'stop-keep-going-checkin-signals',
      'psych-verbal-signaling',
      'psych-nonverbal-signaling'
