  (
    'core-toy-position-planning-map',
    'Toy Position Planning Map',
    'toy-position-planning-map',
    'Connects position curiosity, location context, toys, gear, shopping, hygiene, and partner reassurance.',
    'When a worksheet asks about positions or toys, turn it into planning fields: already tried, curious, no, location fit, surface needs, privacy, stamina, flexibility, toy type, cleaning, charging, storage, material safety, partner reassurance, and whether the toy is solo, shared, or assistive. The relationship beat is often trust: nobody is being replaced; they are building a more precise shared toolkit.',
    'Do not imply toys are required for good sex or that a partner is inadequate without them. Do not ignore hygiene, material, or safety concerns.',
    array[
      'bedroom-bucket-list-position-marking',
      'toy-interest-partner-workload-caveat',
      'sex-toy-equipment-safety-table',
      'toy-gear-hygiene-menu',
      'phys-comfort-constraints'
    ],
    array['sex toys', 'positions', 'planning']
  ),
  (
    'core-sexual-vibe-debrief-map',
    'Sexual Vibe Debrief Map',
    'sexual-vibe-debrief-map',
    'Uses deeper intimacy questions and worksheet exchange to build repair, memory, fear, satisfaction, and next-step planning.',
    'Debrief questions should create tenderness and useful friction. Buckets: desired frequency, what makes sex good, how each person expresses their sexual side, satisfaction, skill insecurity, partner praise, requested improvement, favorite sexual memory, biggest fear, and the difference between sex and making love. After exchange, ask what surprised them, what changed their understanding, what communication needs changing, and what next step feels wanted by both.',
    'Do not turn debrief into a performance review with a winner. Keep criticism specific, kind, and actionable.',
    array[
      'sexual-vibe-deep-dive-questions',
