values
  (
    'core-character-introductions-source-router',
    'Character Introductions Source Router',
    'character-introductions-source-router',
    'Routes character-introduction advice into opening-scene craft, first-impression design, and revision feedback without duplicating the broader intro taxonomy.',
    'Use this pack when a source discusses first appearances, opening scenes, or early reader attachment. Treat the source as craft guidance: introduce the character through pressure, choice, context, and emotional signal rather than biography or static description.',
    'Do not dump backstory, freeze the opening for explanation, or treat the article as a rigid formula. Keep the first impression active, specific, and revisable.',
    array[
      'character-introductions-source',
      'character-introduction-anchor-map',
      'scene-purpose',
      'protagonist-empathy-hook'
    ],
    array['character introductions', 'opening scene', 'first impression']
  ),
  (
    'core-opening-character-intro-scene-choice-map',
    'Opening Character Intro Scene Choice Map',
    'opening-character-intro-scene-choice-map',
    'Turns a character introduction into a scene-function check: movement, setup, theme, tone, and reader interest.',
    'When designing an introduction, ask what the scene does besides placing the character onstage. It should move the story forward, set up the next pressure point, reveal usable character truth, reinforce theme or emotional arc, and give the reader a reason to stay.',
    'Do not open with neutral routine unless the routine is already under strain. Avoid throat-clearing, static biography, or action that arrives before the reader has any reason to care.',
    array[
      'opening-character-intro-scene-choice',
      'scene-purpose',
      'theme-scene-pressure',
      'intro-genre-setting-mood-tone'
    ],
    array['opening check', 'scene purpose', 'first scene']
  ),
  (
    'core-context-not-backstory-intro-map',
    'Context, Not Backstory Intro Map',
    'context-not-backstory-intro-map',
    'Separates useful opening context from exposition-heavy backstory.',
    'Give the reader the minimum context needed to understand the current pressure: where the character is, what problem is alive, what they want right now, and what their reaction tells us. Let history show through behavior, avoidance, objects, stakes, and friction.',
    'Do not explain the whole life story before the scene has earned it. Avoid passive background summary when action, dialogue, or a pressured choice can carry the same information.',
    array[
      'opening-context-not-backstory',
      'intro-conflict-in-action',
      'character-description-by-action',
      'scene-purpose'
    ],
    array['context', 'backstory restraint', 'in action']
  ),
  (
    'core-reader-impression-empathy-map',
    'Reader Impression and Empathy Map',
    'reader-impression-empathy-map',
    'Builds a first impression around empathy, likability, compulsion, vulnerability, longing, and false belief.',
    'Shape the first impression by deciding what the reader should notice and feel: the character''s pressure, contradiction, tenderness, sharp edge, private want, or protective lie. Make them vivid enough to follow before asking the reader to understand everything.',
    'Do not sand the character flat to make them likable. Avoid announcing the wound or flaw in abstract terms before the scene has shown its cost.',
    array[
      'reader-first-impression-control',
      'empathy-likeable-compelling-triad',
      'intro-vulnerability-longing-false-belief',
      'protagonist-empathy-hook'
    ],
