    ],
    array['JanitorAI', 'SillyTavern', 'MarinaraTavern']
  ),
  (
    'core-pov-visceral-reaction-map',
    'POV Visceral Reaction Map',
    'pov-visceral-reaction-map',
    'Descriptive vocabulary',
    'Uses internal body sensation for viewpoint emotion while preserving POV limits and modern prose restraint.',
    'For the viewpoint character, favor felt body response over visible self-description. Track where the feeling lands: throat, jaw, chest, belly, skin, hands, breath, eyes, muscles, pulse, balance, temperature, saliva, nausea, ache, numbness, or pressure. Use clusters only for high intensity. Avoid symptom stuffing. The best line names the body effect and lets the reader infer the emotion, unless naming it adds clarity or voice.',
    array[
      'visceral-reaction-pov',
      'pov-perception-boundary',
      'phys-physiological-response',
      'phys-sensory-reference',
      'psych-body-continuity',
      'emotion-description-bank'
    ],
    array['JanitorAI', 'SillyTavern', 'MarinaraTavern']
  ),
  (
    'core-observed-body-language-cue-map',
    'Observed Body Language Cue Map',
    'observed-body-language-cue-map',
    'Descriptive vocabulary',
    'Classifies visible emotional cues without treating body language as proof or repeating novice beats.',
    'For non-POV characters, use visible cues: posture, hands, mouth, eyes, shoulders, stance, distance, object handling, pace, stillness, interruption, or deliberate movement. Read cues as scene evidence, not certainty. Pair them with context, baseline, relationship history, and contradiction. Watch for overused beats. Replace generic smile/frown/shrug/nod with action that changes the room: a cup set down too carefully, a sleeve pulled over a wrist, a joke abandoned halfway.',
    array[
      'observed-body-language-cues',
      'overused-body-cue-warning',
      'pov-perception-boundary',
      'psych-body-language',
      'eye-mouth-hands-cue-vocabulary',
      'emotion-description-bank'
    ],
    array['JanitorAI', 'SillyTavern', 'MarinaraTavern']
  ),
  (
    'core-dialogue-tone-emotion-map',
    'Dialogue Tone Emotion Map',
    'dialogue-tone-emotion-map',
    'Dialogue',
    'Routes emotion through speech pattern, voice texture, silence, tag restraint, and character-specific diction.',
    'Dialogue can show emotion through pace, clipped answers, overexplaining, softness, sharpness, jokes, deflection, repetition, unfinished sentences, politeness under strain, or the word the character refuses to say. Voice cues should be used sparingly and preferably when observed by the viewpoint. Replace adverb-heavy tags with beats that carry pressure. Dirty, romantic, angry, or frightened speech should still sound like the speaker, not like a generic mood label.',
    array[
      'dialogue-emotion-cue',
      'tone-of-voice-emotion',
      'interior-thought-compression',
      'psych-character-true-dialogue',
      'erotic-vocabulary-calibration',
      'emotion-description-bank'
    ],
    array['JanitorAI', 'SillyTavern', 'MarinaraTavern']
  ),
  (
    'core-mood-description-simile-map',
    'Mood Description Simile Map',
    'mood-description-simile-map',
    'Descriptive vocabulary',
    'Uses setting, object detail, verb choice, and POV-grounded comparison to carry emotion without melodrama.',
    'Filter description through the viewpoint character''s mood. Choose verbs and concrete details that tilt the room without announcing the feeling. A setting can feel generous, hostile, stale, seductive, watched, cramped, or merciful depending on what the character notices. Similes should come from the character''s memory, work, class, culture, fear, desire, or imagination. Avoid billboard metaphors; let a small word choice do the work.',
    array[
      'mood-filtered-description',
      'pov-grounded-simile',
      'theme-motif-symbol-engine',
      'vocab-sensory-perception',
      'pov-perception-boundary',
      'emotion-description-bank'
    ],
    array['JanitorAI', 'SillyTavern', 'MarinaraTavern']
  ),
  (
    'core-hidden-emotion-subtext-map',
    'Hidden Emotion Subtext Map',
    'hidden-emotion-subtext-map',
    'Descriptive vocabulary',
    'Handles suppressed, denied, secret, and faked emotion through contradiction and tiny leaks.',
    'For hidden emotion, write the mismatch. A character may force a smile, square their shoulders, soften their voice, change posture, swallow the first answer, or overcorrect after a small involuntary cue. For denial, use faint physical discomfort or a practical explanation that does not quite fit. For secret emotion, let the first impulse start and then stop. For faked emotion, let words and body disagree. Keep it light; subtext dies when every cue explains itself.',
    array[
      'suppressed-secret-faked-emotion',
      'dialogue-emotion-cue',
      'tone-of-voice-emotion',
      'observed-body-language-cues',
      'concealed-emotional-investment',
      'psych-social-mask'
    ],
    array['JanitorAI', 'SillyTavern', 'MarinaraTavern']
  ),
  (
    'core-emotional-intensity-layering-map',
    'Emotional Intensity Layering Map',
    'emotional-intensity-layering-map',
    'Scene craft',
    'Builds emotional movement through intensity curves, cue density, rest beats, and layered contradictions.',
    'Emotions should move. Map the scene from first flicker to full height to release, residue, or changed feeling. Use mild cues for low intensity, tighter clusters for stronger intensity, and rest beats so the reader is not held at maximum voltage for too long. Layer emotion rather than replacing one label with another: want plus resentment, fear plus pride, desire plus caution, tenderness plus anger. The richest scenes let the dominant emotion change because new information enters the room.',
    array[
      'emotional-intensity-curve',
      'layered-emotion-choreography',
      'psych-pacing-escalation',
      'theme-scene-pressure',
      'phys-physiological-response',
      'emotion-description-bank'
    ],
    array['JanitorAI', 'SillyTavern', 'MarinaraTavern']
  ),
  (
    'core-reader-emotion-pov-engine',
    'Reader Emotion POV Engine',
    'reader-emotion-pov-engine',
    'Scene craft',
