  default_platform_targets
)
values
  (
    'core-story-stakes-taxonomy-router',
    'Story Stakes Taxonomy Router',
    'story-stakes-taxonomy-router',
    'Plot craft',
    'Routes story-stakes craft into consequence, tension, escalation, genre promise, and revision diagnostics.',
    'Use stakes as the pressure that makes a character keep moving. Define the goal, the negative consequence of failure, who pays the price, why walking away is not emotionally or materially possible, and how the consequence changes over time. Keep the language direct and modern: stakes are not hype; they are loss with teeth. Good stakes change choices, deepen conflict, and give the ending something real to prove.',
    array[
      'story-stakes-source',
      'negative-consequence-stakes',
      'theme-scene-pressure',
      'real-choice-negative-consequence',
      'psych-pacing-escalation',
      'playable-handoff'
    ],
    array['JanitorAI', 'SillyTavern', 'MarinaraTavern']
  ),
  (
    'core-eleven-stakes-type-map',
    'Eleven Stakes Type Map',
    'eleven-stakes-type-map',
    'Plot craft',
    'Groups the guide''s stake types into story-ready consequence families.',
    'Classify stakes by consequence family: general protection, demise, livelihood, freedom, reputation, sanity or consciousness, access, regret, suffering, sacrifice, justice, and happiness. Many stories layer more than one. Use safety-level stakes when the plot asks for physical danger, captivity, survival, or social ruin. Use spiritual-level stakes when the plot turns on love, esteem, justice, reputation, or fulfillment. Use contingency stakes to make victory mean that the pain was not wasted.',
    array[
      'general-protection-stakes',
      'demise-stakes',
      'livelihood-freedom-stakes',
      'reputation-access-stakes',
      'sanity-consciousness-stakes',
      'regret-suffering-sacrifice-stakes',
      'justice-happiness-stakes'
    ],
    array['JanitorAI', 'SillyTavern', 'MarinaraTavern']
  ),
  (
    'core-emotional-stakes-modulator-map',
    'Emotional Stakes Modulator Map',
    'emotional-stakes-modulator-map',
    'Plot craft',
    'Maps why the same consequence can feel faint, sharp, unbearable, funny, romantic, or earned.',
    'Modulate stakes through emotional bond, restriction, vulnerable-dependency caution, backstory echo, setting pressure, and contingency. A reader or player cares more when they know the person at risk, have seen the future that might vanish, understand the old wound being reopened, feel the deadline closing, or see that the cost has already been paid. Avoid cheap escalation. The modulator should make the consequence more intimate, not merely louder.',
    array[
      'emotional-bond-stakes-modulator',
      'boundaries-restrictions-modulator',
      'vulnerable-dependent-stakes-caveat',
      'backstory-setting-stakes-modulator',
      'regret-suffering-sacrifice-stakes',
      'stakes-reminder-strategy'
    ],
    array['JanitorAI', 'SillyTavern', 'MarinaraTavern']
  ),
  (
    'core-cost-vs-need-stakes-matrix',
    'Cost Versus Need Stakes Matrix',
    'cost-vs-need-stakes-matrix',
    'Plot craft',
    'Checks whether the character''s risk level matches the need, consequence, genre, and tone.',
    'For each major action, compare cost against stakes. Low cost plus spiritual need suits comedy, courtship, embarrassment, and social pressure. Low cost plus safety need can create quick tension without heavy danger. High cost plus safety need can sustain danger, thriller, adventure, and dark romance pressure. High cost plus spiritual need needs extra support: justice, reputation, love, or happiness must be sold hard enough that the risk does not look foolish. If the cost outruns the stakes, add stronger stakes or reduce the danger.',
    array[
      'cost-of-participation-matrix',
      'anticlimax-stakes-alignment',
      'negative-consequence-stakes',
      'theme-scene-pressure',
      'external-action-internal-growth',
      'character-arc-revision-checklist'
    ],
    array['JanitorAI', 'SillyTavern', 'MarinaraTavern']
  ),
  (
    'core-romance-stakes-escalation-map',
    'Romance Stakes Escalation Map',
    'romance-stakes-escalation-map',
    'Romance craft',
    'Adapts stakes craft to romance, romantic comedy, and relationship-driven roleplay.',
    'In romance, stakes often begin as happiness, self-respect, belonging, reputation, family pressure, or access to love. Escalation comes from commitment, vulnerability, intimacy, sacrifice, public choice, and the moment one character risks the protective habit that kept them safe. The midpoint should make losing the relationship hurt more than it would have earlier. The climax should ask for a visible cost: apology, confession, boundary, refusal, public stand, leaving the safer option, or choosing love without erasing selfhood.',
    array[
      'justice-happiness-stakes',
      'reputation-access-stakes',
      'regret-suffering-sacrifice-stakes',
      'romance-viewpoint-stakes',
      'relationship-double-arc',
      'psych-intimacy-vulnerability'
    ],
    array['JanitorAI', 'SillyTavern', 'MarinaraTavern']
  ),
  (
    'core-stakes-qc-anticlimax-check',
    'Stakes QC Anticlimax Check',
    'stakes-qc-anticlimax-check',
    'Revision',
    'Revision pass for weak stakes, unsupported risk, flat middles, and climaxes that feel loud but hollow.',
    'Run a stakes QC pass scene by scene. Ask: what can be lost, who loses it, why now, why can this not wait, what blocks easy exit, what has already been sacrificed, and what changes if the character fails? Watch for common failures: danger without reason, romance without vulnerability, justice without personal cost, reputation that does not affect life, suffering that never accumulates, and a climax that forgets the threatened person or promise. Repair by making the consequence concrete, personal, time-bound, and visible.',
    array[
      'anticlimax-stakes-alignment',
      'cost-of-participation-matrix',
      'stakes-reminder-strategy',
      'character-arc-revision-checklist',
      'leap-of-faith-climax',
      'denouement-arc-proof'
    ],
    array['JanitorAI', 'SillyTavern', 'MarinaraTavern']
  ),
  (
    'core-secondary-stakes-functions-map',
    'Secondary Stakes Functions Map',
    'secondary-stakes-functions-map',
    'Plot craft',
    'Uses stakes for openings, antagonists, pacing, suspects, red herrings, and believable reversals.',
    'Stakes do more than drive the central goal. Use short-term opening stakes to pull the reader in before the main plot is named. Give antagonists something to lose so they feel human without excusing harm. Use reminders of the stakes to vary pacing and recharge emotion. In mysteries, ask what a suspect protects by lying: freedom, marriage, reputation, access, livelihood, or shame. For sudden cooperation or betrayal, add a new stake that makes the reversal feel pressured rather than convenient.',
    array[
      'stakes-reminder-strategy',
      'negative-consequence-stakes',
      'reputation-access-stakes',
      'livelihood-freedom-stakes',
      'psych-character-flaw',
      'theme-scene-pressure'
    ],
    array['JanitorAI', 'SillyTavern', 'MarinaraTavern']
  )
