    'Routes the source into orgasm/ejaculation distinction, arousal pacing, body awareness, partner disclosure, and safety caveats.',
    'Use this source as adult body-response and belief-source taxonomy, not as medical advice. Capture the useful distinctions: orgasm can be written separately from ejaculation; arousal can rise, hover, drop, return, or collapse; recovery may be physical, emotional, awkward, proud, or frustrating. Keep the prose modern and humane. Strip out the guru tone, miracle claims, and gender certainty. In fiction, the strongest material is the character trying to read his own body while also managing pride, vulnerability, partner care, and the fear of failing at something he wants to master.',
    array[
      'male-multiple-orgasm-source',
      'non-ejaculatory-orgasm-claim',
      'orgasm-ejaculation-distinction',
      'arousal-threshold-pacing',
      'sex-technique-medical-caveat'
