    'Self-Esteem Popularity Prestige Economy Map',
    'self-esteem-popularity-prestige-economy-map',
    'Social-resource taxonomy',
    'Maps confidence, reputation, estate assets, and luck into a usable story economy.',
    'Track four social resources. Self-esteem is private force: nerve, focus, stubbornness, and the ability to keep affecting the story. Popularity is public force: who listens, who follows, who repeats the story afterward. Prestige is durable standing: estate assets, loyal staff, libraries, magical help, titles, and visible signs of rule. Luck is spendable sparkle: a small miracle earned through delight, generosity, or timing. Pressure lands cleanly when a scene makes the character choose which resource to spend and which part of them pays for it.',
    array[
      'self-esteem-popularity-resource',
      'prestige-estate-resource',
      'luck-dessert-economy',
      'status-power-distinction',
      'prestige-respect-deference'
    ],
    array['JanitorAI', 'SillyTavern', 'MarinaraTavern']
  ),
  (
    'core-positive-drawback-trait-engine',
    'Positive Drawback Trait Engine',
    'positive-drawback-trait-engine',
    'Character design taxonomy',
    'Builds traits that help and hinder at the same time, giving characters immediate playable texture.',
    'Write traits as pressure-bearing contradictions. A trait should offer a real advantage, then reveal the bill. Examples of the pattern: generous but easily flattered; fearless but careless with other people''s fear; beloved but terrified of disappointing a crowd; elegant but slow to improvise; clever but addicted to proving it. In roleplay, trigger the benefit when the character leans into their nature and trigger the drawback when the same nature meets bad timing, social pressure, or a sharper opponent.',
    array[
      'positive-drawback-trait',
      'protagonist-flaw-pressure',
      'character-description-by-action',
      'character-dossier-backstory',
      'playable-handoff'
    ],
    array['JanitorAI', 'SillyTavern', 'MarinaraTavern']
  ),
  (
    'core-fairy-castle-estate-setting-map',
    'Fairy Castle and Estate Setting Map',
    'fairy-castle-estate-setting-map',
    'Worldbuilding taxonomy',
    'Separates the everyday estate world from the fairy-maintained portal arena where stylized conflict can happen.',
    'Use the setting split to keep consequences legible. The estates hold politics, resources, staff, borders, reputation, and public life. The Castle holds ritualized contests, fairy interference, strange rules, and suspended danger. Portals let characters move from courtly pressure into symbolic conflict and back again. Fairies should not be decoration only; give them patient agendas, obscure etiquette, bargains, tests, or a sense of humor that makes every contest feel lightly haunted by another intelligence.',
