    'Scene Structure Router',
    'scene-structure-router',
    'Scene craft taxonomy',
    'Routes scenes through purpose, intrigue, clarity, direction, opening, development, ending, and consequence.',
    'Before writing or evaluating a scene, name what the scene changes. A strong scene has purpose, intrigue, clarity, and direction. Purpose tells why this moment belongs in the story. Intrigue gives the reader a live question. Clarity keeps cause and effect readable. Direction leaves the story in a new state. The scene may be quiet or explosive, but it should not leave the characters, relationship, plot, or reader exactly where they started.',
    array[
      'scene-purpose',
      'scene-intrigue-question',
      'scene-clarity-causality',
      'scene-direction-consequence',
      'scene_function',
      'playable-handoff'
    ],
    array['JanitorAI', 'SillyTavern', 'MarinaraTavern']
  ),
  (
    'core-scene-opening-hook-map',
    'Scene Opening Hook Map',
    'scene-opening-hook-map',
    'Scene craft taxonomy',
    'Builds openings that anchor the reader while creating immediate tension or curiosity.',
    'Open a scene with a handle the reader can grip: who is present, where we are, what has shifted, what tone rules the room, or what question is now unavoidable. A good hook does not need spectacle. It can be a changed expression, an odd setting detail, a sentence said too casually, a missing person, a bad silence, or a choice already in motion. Keep the opening clean enough to orient and charged enough to pull.',
    array[
      'scene-opening-hook',
      'scene-purpose',
      'scene-intrigue-question',
      'theme-scene-pressure',
      'phys-spatial-awareness',
      'psych-tension-anticipation'
    ],
    array['JanitorAI', 'SillyTavern', 'MarinaraTavern']
  ),
  (
    'core-scene-development-complication-map',
    'Scene Development and Complication Map',
    'scene-development-complication-map',
    'Scene craft taxonomy',
    'Turns the middle of a scene into motive, contrast, escalation, and uncertainty rather than filler.',
    'Develop a scene by letting the opening action reveal more than it first promised. Build from want to response, response to complication, complication to changed stakes. Useful middle turns include misread motive, opposing desire, new information, private emotion leaking through behavior, a plan becoming harder, a choice narrowing, or two characters realizing they mean different things by the same words. The middle should make the ending harder, cleaner, or more dangerous.',
    array[
      'scene-development-complication',
      'internal-external-conflict-braid',
      'scene-clarity-causality',
      'psych-character-flaw',
      'theme-scene-pressure',
      'psych-conflict-repair'
    ],
    array['JanitorAI', 'SillyTavern', 'MarinaraTavern']
  ),
  (
    'core-scene-ending-turn-map',
    'Scene Ending Turn Map',
    'scene-ending-turn-map',
    'Scene craft taxonomy',
    'Shapes scene endings that close one beat while opening the next question or pressure.',
    'End a scene when something has turned. The turn may be a decision, reveal, refusal, arrival, exit, wound, joke that lands wrong, promise, proof, new risk, or quiet realization. A scene ending should feel complete enough to satisfy and unfinished enough to pull. Avoid stopping only because the conversation runs out. Leave with altered knowledge, altered feeling, altered danger, or altered distance between characters.',
    array[
      'scene-ending-turn',
      'scene-direction-consequence',
      'scene-sequel-continuity',
      'playable-handoff',
      'chapter-state-transition-map',
      'theme-scene-pressure'
    ],
    array['JanitorAI', 'SillyTavern', 'MarinaraTavern']
  ),
  (
    'core-scene-continuity-qc-loop',
    'Scene Continuity QC Loop',
    'scene-continuity-qc-loop',
    'Scene craft taxonomy',
    'Checks whether a scene earns its place, reads clearly, and creates consequence for the next beat.',
    'QC a scene with four questions. Purpose: what would break if this scene vanished? Intrigue: what does the reader want to know by the end of the first beat? Clarity: can we follow who acts, why it matters, and what caused the turn? Direction: what must the next scene answer or carry forward? If the answer is vague, sharpen the scene around a choice, cost, reveal, reversal, or relationship-state change.',
