    'Use campus Greek-life material as social-world architecture, not as a blanket verdict. Buckets: chapter identity, house reputation, brotherhood or sisterhood language, rush, pledge, initiation, legacy, networking, second-family appeal, elitism, party culture, alcohol pressure, conformity, dues, time cost, hazing risk, misogyny risk, and reform or anti-hazing policy. For fiction, the useful question is what the group offers, what it demands, who is protected, who is excluded, and what a character is willing to ignore to belong.',
